<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!--
	The one source of truth for backend Java formatting. Read by TWO consumers,
	which is the whole point of picking the Eclipse JDT engine:

	  * Spotless, via <eclipse><file> in pom.xml   -> `make format-check/-apply`
	  * VS Code, via "java.format.settings.url" in ../.vscode/settings.json

	The Java extension's formatter IS Eclipse JDT, so pointing both at this file
	makes format-on-save agree with the build instead of fighting it. A
	space-based formatter (google-java-format, palantir-java-format) could not
	do this: neither exposes an indent-character setting, and both emit spaces
	unconditionally.

	Deviations from Eclipse's built-in defaults, and only these:
	  tabulation.char   = tab    (was: space)  (repo preference)
	  lineSplit         = 160    (was: 120)
	  comment line len  = 160    (was: 80)
	  join_wrapped_lines= false  (was: true)   (see below)
	  join_lines_in_comments = false

	`join_wrapped_lines=false` is load-bearing. Left at Eclipse's default
	`true`, the formatter RE-JOINS lines the author split deliberately, so the
	one-time reformat would rewrite far more than the 40 files that actually
	have a line over 160 columns. Same reasoning for comments.

	The `keep_*_on_one_line` settings matter more than they look, and were
	measured rather than guessed: at Eclipse's DEFAULT the formatter explodes
	every compact accessor - `public Map<..> getX() { return x; }` into three
	lines, `public Partition() { }` into two - which made 125 of 206 files
	differ even under `diff -w`. This repo is full of one-line accessors and a
	formatting pass has no business reflowing them.

	Careful when editing that family: JDT validates each key against its OWN
	whitelist, not a shared one, and a bad value makes the whole profile fail
	to load ("Unrecognized value for setting ..."). Verified against the JDT
	bytecode:
	  * the 5 `*_block_*` / `switch_case_with_arrow` keys accept ONLY
	    `one_line_never` and `one_line_if_empty`
	  * the declaration keys (method_body, type, enum, record, annotation, ...)
	    also accept `one_line_preserve`, `one_line_always`,
	    `one_line_if_single_item`
	  * `keep_simple_getter_setter_on_one_line`, `keep_guardian_clause...`,
	    `keep_simple_if...` and `keep_empty_array_initializer...` are plain
	    BOOLEANS and reject every `one_line_*` value
-->
